r you're selling I don't care how  it works I just want to know that it  works then I get whatever benefit from  it so focus on the market inside focus  on the benefits and not the specifics  that makes it harder to reverse-engineer  makes it harder for somebody else  come up and say I'm going to copy them  exactly so you don't have to share all  the schematics and diagrams and the  technical details so that will protect  you the second way is ideally you want a  patent ok the patent gives you solid  protection but the first step typically  is a provisional pen and provisional  patents are easier to get it gives you  wider coverage and are not nearly as  expensive and there's no annual  maintenance fee what it does is  basically the idea for Bishop  provisional patents is to give  entrepreneurs and inventors a year to  test out the idea so to see can I  commercialize this can I make money from  this and after that yours up you have to  decide am I going to go and get the  patent or not and spend all the money on  it so what you could do is if you think  you have this great idea you get a  provisional patent for it you put it up  on Kickstarter you have a year to go out  and make money and raise money and then  get some sales and see if this is  something that people really want and if  you're making money from it then you can  go and afford to get the patent if it  totally dies on you know there's zero  interest nothing is happening then you  have to make the tough call of after  that year do you want to go for the full  patent or not keep in mind though with a  patent you still have to enforce it the  most expensive part about a patent is  enforcing it because people can look at  what you're doing  and do it themselves and then you have  to go sue them to say you can't do this  you can't steal my idea and you don't  have typically two pockets entrepreneurs  don't have the pockets to do it the way  to make money with a patent is to  typically license it or sell it t